<!-- §01 ─ Problem ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The O(L³) Wall</div>
<h2>Why diffusion LLMs are computationally brutal</h2>
<p>
  Autoregressive models generate tokens left-to-right, one at a time. Each step is O(L) and they cache key-value activations once per token — so total generation cost is O(L²). Diffusion LLMs work differently: they start with a fully masked sequence and iteratively unmask tokens over T denoising steps. Every step must process <em>all L tokens</em> with full bidirectional attention. No KV-cache. Total cost: <strong>O(L² × T)</strong> — effectively O(L³) since T scales with L.
</p>
<p>
  For L=512 with T=64 steps, that's 512 × 512 × 64 = 16.8 billion operations. Compare to the final autoregressive forward pass: 512 × 512 = 262K. The gap is staggering. This is why LLaDA-8B achieves only 8–10 tokens per second on a high-end GPU — about 10× slower than comparable autoregressive models.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">compute-cost — AR vs dLLM for L=512 tokens, T=64 steps</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-compute" width="600" height="200" style="width:100%;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The root problem is that each denoising step must re-evaluate all token representations from scratch. Unlike autoregressive models where a token's key-value pair is computed once and cached, diffusion LLMs update tokens continuously across steps — a token unmasked at step 15 gets re-evaluated at step 14, 13, 12, and so on. Prior acceleration methods tried static caching (reuse cached features for "stable" tokens) or fixed-threshold parallel decoding (unmask multiple tokens per step when confidence exceeds τ). Both use the same strategy for every layer and every step — and both ignore how differently each layer actually behaves.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 ─ Layer Dynamics ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-layer" data-code="layer">
<div class="section-no">02 — Layers Have Different Update Needs</div>
<h2>Shallow layers are stable; deep layers are dynamic</h2>
<p>
  The key insight behind Dynamic Cache Updating: token representations change at vastly different rates depending on which layer you're in. The authors measured how often tokens' internal features change between consecutive decoding steps and found a clear pattern — shallow layers (1–12) are highly stable, while deep layers (20–32) fluctuate significantly at every step.
</p>
<p>
  Existing methods apply the same cache-update budget to every layer. This wastes compute on stable shallow layers (refreshing things that haven't changed) while under-investing in dynamic deep layers (missing important updates). The fix: measure each layer's actual dynamism and allocate budget proportionally.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">layer-budget — cache update allocation by layer</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to compare Static vs DCU allocation</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="mode-btns">
      <button class="mode-btn active" data-layer-mode="static" id="btn-layer-static">Static (Uniform)</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-layer-mode="dcu" id="btn-layer-dcu">DCU (Dynamic)</button>
      <span class="mode-annotation" id="layer-annotation">Same budget for every layer — wastes compute on stable layers</span>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-layer" width="600" height="240" style="width:100%;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  DCU measures token-level dissimilarity at each layer using cosine distance between inputs at adjacent steps. Layers with high average dissimilarity get a proportionally larger update budget. Crucially, computing input-level similarity is cheap — the authors show that layer inputs correlate strongly with downstream activations (Key, Value, Attention Output, FFN Output all have Spearman ρ ≥ 0.94), so the proxy is accurate without extra forward passes.
</p>
<p>
  One edge case: if a token gets skipped for updates across multiple layers (its input doesn't change because it wasn't updated upstream), its variation score drops to zero and it may get starved of updates indefinitely — "stuck in the mud." DCU addresses this with a <strong>Mandatory Update Window</strong>: a fixed-size neighborhood around the "key token" (the highest-confidence token about to be unmasked) is always updated, regardless of the adaptive budget.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 ─ Threshold Trap ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-threshold" data-code="threshold">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Threshold Trap</div>
<h2>Confidence fluctuates — a fixed threshold commits at the wrong time</h2>
<p>
  Parallel decoding speeds up diffusion LLMs by unmasking multiple tokens per step: any token whose confidence exceeds a fixed threshold τ (e.g., 0.7) gets committed in the current step. But confidence isn't monotonic. A token can be 82% confident at step 15, drop to 34% at step 12 as surrounding context changes, then stabilize at 95% at step 4. A fixed threshold would commit it too early — with the wrong value.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">confidence-trajectory — token confidence across 16 decoding steps</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle to see when commitment happens</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="mode-btns">
      <button class="mode-btn active" data-thresh-mode="fixed" id="btn-thresh-fixed">Fixed Threshold (τ=0.70)</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-thresh-mode="adaptive" id="btn-thresh-adaptive">Adaptive Threshold (APD)</button>
      <span class="mode-annotation" id="thresh-annotation">Commits at step 11 → WRONG (confidence drops to 0.34 after)</span>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-thresh" width="600" height="260" style="width:100%;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The paper's observation: the gap between the top prediction and the runner-up is a better signal than absolute confidence. When a token's distribution is concentrated (large gap between #1 and #2 probabilities), it's stable even if the absolute confidence is moderate. When the distribution is diffuse, commitment should be delayed — even if absolute confidence looks high.
</p>
<p>
  APD also tracks <em>temporal instability</em>: the cosine distance between a token's confidence distributions at consecutive steps. Large shifts signal that the model is still updating its prediction — raise the threshold. Small shifts signal stability — lower the threshold for early commitment.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Adaptive Parallel Decoding — threshold update rule</div>
  <span class="math-var">τᵢᵗ</span> = <span class="math-var">τᵢᵗ⁺¹</span> − <span class="math-var">α</span>·<span class="math-var">cᵢᵗ</span> + <span class="math-var">β</span>·<span class="math-var">Hᵢᵗ</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">τᵢᵗ = threshold for token i at step t</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">cᵢᵗ = confidence concentration = 1 − (second-highest probability)</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">  → large cᵢᵗ means peaked distribution → lower threshold (commit early)</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">Hᵢᵗ = cosine distance between confidence at steps t and t+1</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">  → large Hᵢᵗ means unstable prediction → raise threshold (hold off)</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §04 ─ Method ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-method" data-code="method">
<div class="section-no">04 — Dynamic-dLLM: Two Components</div>
<h2>DCU + APD: a unified adaptive framework</h2>

<div class="arch-svg-wrap">
