<p>
  Both components work together seamlessly. DCU runs during the transformer forward pass, controlling which tokens' cached features get refreshed per layer. APD runs after the forward pass, using the output logits to decide which tokens are confident enough to unmask in the current step. Neither component requires any gradient computation — just two extra operations on existing activations.
</p>
<p>
  The mandatory update window in DCU ensures that the neighborhood around the most confident token (the one APD is most likely to commit) always gets fresh activations, preventing cascade errors from stale caches.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 ─ Budget Tradeoff ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-tradeoff" data-code="tradeoff">
<div class="section-no">05 — Cache Budget: Speed vs Accuracy Tradeoff</div>
<h2>B<sub>layer</sub>=32 hits the sweet spot</h2>
<p>
  The two key hyperparameters in DCU are <strong>B<sub>layer</sub></strong> (the per-layer cache update budget — how many tokens get their cache refreshed per layer per step) and <strong>B<sub>window</sub></strong> (the mandatory update window size around the key token). Both control the accuracy/throughput tradeoff.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">budget-tradeoff — accuracy and throughput vs B_layer on LLaDA-8B-Instruct (GSM8K)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a budget to highlight</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-budget" width="600" height="240" style="width:100%;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div id="budget-note" style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono);margin-top:.5rem;text-align:center">
      B_layer=32 — accuracy plateau, throughput peak — chosen as default
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Larger B<sub>layer</sub> means more tokens get fresh cache updates → higher accuracy but lower throughput (more compute per step). The ablation shows accuracy reaches a plateau around B<sub>layer</sub>=32 while throughput drops rapidly above it. The window B<sub>window</sub> has a similar shape but smaller B<sub>window</sub> causes steeper accuracy drops — the mandatory window is critical for preventing "stuck in the mud" tokens from corrupting the entire generation.
</p>
<p>
  The adaptive threshold in APD can reduce inference steps by approximately <strong>30%</strong> compared to a fixed threshold with the same initialization at τ=0.9, achieving the same accuracy while finishing earlier. This is because the adaptive threshold quickly drops for tokens that have stabilized, enabling earlier commitment without the error rate of a globally low fixed threshold.
</p>
</section>

